Maintain a stable ordering without losing occurrences (@computing-order-contract: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Specify-all-matches-and-verify-a-linear-scan.lingua#r_005C1A828D74737220FF261B83") { r_06F37ABB974E19CF35EB730FCA
An ordered result must satisfy more than adjacent keys being nondecreasing. It must contain exactly the original occurrences, and our contract also preserves input order among equal keys. This last property is stability. For `[("b", 20), ("a", 10), ("b", 21)]`, the required order is `[("a", 10), ("b", 20), ("b", 21)]`. Sorting by both key and payload would impose an additional rule; it is not equivalent when the two b payloads arrive in the opposite order.

Here keys are exact nonempty strings. Comparison uses Python's lexical string ordering; it does not infer numeric identifiers, case folding, locale collation or Unicode normalization. Thus `"0012"` and `"12"` are distinct keys. A different application may deliberately normalize keys, but then collisions and lost distinctions become part of its specification. The payload does not participate in ordering.

Build an ordered prefix by inserting each next item. Suppose positions before i already contain the input prefix in stable key order. Save item i separately and treat its old position as a hole. Move each strictly greater preceding key one place right, moving the hole left. Stop at the beginning or immediately after a key less than or equal to the saved key, then fill the hole. For `b20, a10, b21, c30`, only b20 moves when a10 is inserted. The later b21 stays after b20.

The outer invariant is that the processed prefix is a stable sorted permutation of the same input occurrences and the remaining suffix retains its input values. During insertion, the saved item plus occupied positions retain every occurrence: the temporarily duplicated slot is the conceptual hole, not an additional logical item. Shifted items are strictly greater than the saved key and remain in their previous relative order. On insertion, both neighboring comparisons are satisfied; equal predecessors were never crossed. This establishes preservation of order, multiplicity and stability. Initially a zero- or one-element prefix already satisfies the property. The inner index decreases on every shift; the outer processed length increases to the finite input length. Correctness and termination are separate obligations, as in [Derive a loop from an invariant and a stopping rule](Derive-a-loop-from-an-invariant-and-a-stopping-rule.lingua#r_02552612F644E09F0E388FB4B9).

**Read:** [Introduction to Algorithms — MIT 6.006, Spring 2020](../sources/Introduction-to-Algorithms-MIT-6006.lingua#r_04AEC4B446A81994AEEB84801D) lecture 3's sorting specification and insertion-sort page, after lecture 2's interface preparation. Its recursive presentation and our iterative, instrumented implementation are different expressions of an ordered-prefix argument. Defer merge-sort recurrences until recursion and their mathematical solution are introduced. Use [Python tutorial and standard-library references — Python documentation](../../inquiry/reproducible-computation/sources/Python-Reference.lingua#r_53J5SVX9CRECPQJFX3EAD8NRCZ)'s sorting guide to distinguish `sorted`, in-place list sorting and stability.

**Work:** annotate the hole and saved item for keys `c, b, b, a`. Label occurrences by arrival position even if their payloads are equal. Show how changing the shift condition to include equality reverses some equal-key arrivals. Prove the preserved-occurrence claim rather than checking only the output's order. Explain why a sorted empty result would satisfy an order check but fail the specification for nonempty input.

**Continue:** [Specify all matches and verify a linear scan](Specify-all-matches-and-verify-a-linear-scan.lingua#r_005C1A828D74737220FF261B83).

**Return:** [Computing](../Computing.lingua#r_02FE84C805C79A9D396F478641).
